  "task": "# Relay stage 2: Research\nTask: port-control-server-to-kestrel\nWorking directory: /tmp/nix-shell.L5haGz/visual-relay/wt/fbb5c78605a5/plan-20260707022900/port-control-server-to-kestrel\n\n## Task input\n\n\n## Manifest\n(not set yet)\n\n## Prior stages\n## Stage 1 - Ideate\n\n{ \"summary\": \"Replace System.Net.HttpListener with Kestrel (ASP.NET Core's cross-platform web server) for the embedded localhost control API. The current ControlServer uses HttpListener \u2014 a legacy API with known cross-platform limitations on macOS/Linux. The port must preserve the same lifecycle (Start/Stop/Dispose), route surface (5 endpoints), token auth, and ControlApi business logic while running as an in-process server inside the Avalonia desktop app (WinExe target).\", \"options\": [\"Option A: In-process Kestrel with Minimal API (full WebApplication + endpoint routing) \u2014 idiomatic ASP.NET Core, good testability, but adds ~30+ assemblies to the desktop app.\", \"Option B: Standalone Kestrel host via raw KestrelServer + IConnectionBuilder \u2014 minimal dependency weight (~5 assemblies), but requires manual HTTP parsing from PipeReader/PipeWriter.\", \"Option C: ASP.NET Core Generic Host with UseKestrel + terminal middleware \u2014 gives full HttpContext without MVC/MinimalAPI overhead, keeps existing RouteAsync dispatch pattern, standard IHost lifecycle.\"] }\n\n\n\nEnd your reply with a single fenced ```json block, nothing after it, matching: { \"findings\": string, \"constraints\": string[] }",
