    "answer": "Now I have a thorough understanding of the codebase. Here are my findings:\n\n**Codebase structure**: The Visual Relay app is a .NET 10 Avalonia desktop application (WinExe). The `ControlServer` class in `src/VisualRelay.App/Services/` uses `System.Net.HttpListener` to provide a localhost HTTP control API with 5 endpoints (GET `/`, `/health`, `/state`, `/screenshot[?path=\u2026]`, POST `/command/{name}`). The server lifecycle is Start \u2192 accept loop \u2192 Stop/Dispose. Optional token-based auth uses an `X-VR-Token` header. The routing, response writing (JSON/HTML/PNG), and business logic (ControlApi) are all separate from the transport layer.\n\n**Key transport-surface details**: `ControlServer.cs` (159 lines) runs the accept loop; `ControlServer.Routing.cs` (137 lines) does method+path dispatch, token auth, bodyless-POST guard (411), and response serialization. The bodyless-POST guard is specifically designed around `HttpListener` quirks on macOS/Linux \u2014 it checks raw headers before reading the body. The `Dispose` test (ControlServerTests.cs:181) validates port release by re-binding with a fresh `HttpListener`. Tests use both `HttpClient` (E2E) and raw `TcpClient` (bodyless-POST tests).\n\n**Dependencies**: The App project (`VisualRelay.App.csproj`) currently has zero ASP.NET dependencies \u2014 only Avalonia 12.0.5 packages, CommunityToolkit.Mvvm 8.4.2, and project refs to Core/Domain. It targets `net10.0` with `<OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>`.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"findings\": \"The ControlServer lives at src/VisualRelay.App/Services/ControlServer.cs + ControlServer.Routing.cs, using System.Net.HttpListener for an embedded localhost HTTP API on 127.0.0.1:{port} (default 8765). ControlServerOptions.cs parses env vars VR_CONTROL_DISABLE, VR_CONTROL_PORT, VR_CONTROL_TOKEN. The routing/response layer is tightly coupled to HttpListenerContext (route dispatch, token auth, 411 bodyless-POST guard, WriteJsonAsync/WriteHtmlAsync helper methods that set ContentType/ContentLength64 and write to OutputStream). ControlApi.cs holds the business logic and marshals all VM/window access onto Dispatcher.UIThread. The App.axaml.cs bootstraps with `new ControlServer(new ControlApi(viewModel, window), options).Start()`. Tests (3 files) use HttpClient E2E round-trips and raw TcpClient for the bodyless-POST test, plus a Dispose test that re-binds the port with a fresh HttpListener. The project has no ASP.NET packages yet (target net10.0, WinExe).\",\n  \"constraints\": [\n    \"Must preserve the 5-route surface: GET /, GET /health, GET /state, GET /screenshot[?path=\u2026], POST /command/{name}\",\n    \"Must preserve Start/Stop/Dispose lifecycle with identical exception-swallowing and never-block-startup semantics\",\n    \"Must preserve optional token auth via X-VR-Token header (401 on mismatch)\",\n    \"Must preserve the 411 bodyless-POST guard (no Content-Length + no Transfer-Encoding \u2192 411 before command execution); raw TcpClient tests confirm this behavior\",\n    \"Must preserve response ContentType headers: application/json, text/html; charset=utf-8, image/png\",\n    \"Must preserve X-Screenshot-Path header on screenshot responses\",\n    \"The Dispose test (ControlServer_Dispose_ReleasesListener) currently verifies port release by creating a new HttpListener; will need to be adapted to the new transport\",\n    \"The bodyless-POST test (ControlServerBodylessPostTests) uses raw TcpClient to send malformed HTTP \u2014 must still pass with Kestrel\",\n    \"The App project must remain a WinExe with no external HTTP host process; Kestrel must run in-process\",\n    \"Target framework is net10.0; ASP.NET Core packages must be compatible\",\n    \"All existing tests must pass without behavioral changes to the HTTP API surface\",\n    \"The 300-line-per-test-file guard applies to any modified test files\",\n    \"InternalsVisibleTo is set for VisualRelay.Tests and VisualRelay.Screenshots\",\n    \"The ControlApi constructor signature (MainWindowViewModel, Window, IReadOnlyCollection<string>?) must not change\",\n    \"No dependency on System.Net.HttpListener may remain after the port (it is a banned/legacy API surface)\"\n  ]\n}\n```",
